Well, in my experience C looses against FORTRAN most of the time, but the=
y are
close, usually. If you have some complex stuff, you can write nice and fa=
st C++
programs, though, esp. if you use inline functions efficiently. Often the=
se are
faster than corresponding FORTRAN, since nowadays people usually  don't w=
rite
spaghetti code any more - even if they use FORTRAN77 :-)

But in the end, I would say, speed mostly depends on good algorithms and
efficient implemetations, not on the programming language.

