Yeah, short but nice page. I would still kep my claim, though, based on m=
y
(special ?) personal experiences.
Looking at the programs around me, there usually really is not much of a
difference between programs that are plain FORTRAN or completly C, but th=
e
amount of thought/work people put into their code counts much more.

Btw: My main programming project is a mixed FORTRAN / C++ project, and th=
ats
the way I would suggest for anybody seriously beeing concerned about spee=
d.
Then there remains the problem of which parts to write using this or that
language. You also can use all these nice FORTRAN libs from within C++ cl=
asses,
so you can manipulate your class easily from C++, but it does FORTTRAN in
reality :-)
Here this seems to save development efforts by writing ugly FORTRAN and s=
aves
runtime by using nice programs.
But then an good assembler BLAS is even better :-)

