Neil Carlson wrote:
> 
> Wes Bauske wrote:
> >
> > Greg Lindahl wrote:
> > >
> > > You seem to be assuming that zero is the "right answer". It is
> > > if -mieee is not specified. But if -mieee is specified, then there
> > > should be routines which return a denorm. I'm surprised that CPML
> > > doesn't have such routines. (?)
> > >
> >
> > Zero is the correct answer. If you think a denormal
> > provides useful algorithmic information, you're kidding
> > yourself.
> 
> If you haven't already done so, I'd recommend educating yourself on
> the numerical motivations underlying IEEE 754 by going to W. Kahan's
> website (http://www.cs.berkeley.edu/~wkahan/ieee754status) and doing
> some reading.  Kahan was one of the major framers of the standard
> and is a highly regarded numerical analyst.
> 

Good reference.

This is an interesting statement, note "Gradual Underflow" means denormals:

the troubles caused by underflow cannot all be alleviated by Gradual Underflow; it dispatches only
those
underflows that it can render practically indistinguishable from roundoff. The same effect can be
accomplished by a knowledgeable and diligent
programmer at the cost of a few extra tests against artfully chosen thresholds. Some such tests may
be needed even with Gradual Underflow to render a
program invulnerable to over/underflow. How big a burden should hardware designers bear to save
adept programmers from a few tests, and to protect
some unknowable number of users of naively written programs from falling unwittingly into a tiny
chasm?

Note the comment on naive programmers. Basically, all denormals
do is slightly delay the problem. The problem is still there
however and you need to deal with it in your algorithm.


Wes


