On Tue, 9 Mar 1999, Andrei A. Dergatchev wrote:

> Hi,
> 
> It seems I'm getting close to get my stuff working on my SX.
> But in fact this task requires much more RAM which I need
> and I can afford for my own machine. This task is for project
> and that project has some budget :-) I contacted local
> supercomputer facilities but their requirement to sign
> a contract for 4 years minimum (with price about several $K
> each) doesn't seem terrible attractive to me. So, I'd be
> happy to promote AlphaLinux machine if I'd be able to find
> good prices. There is NO modern Unix machines at all
> in my University which can help so I'll definetely need
> to buy something and the only competitor I see so far
> is a Pentium Linux :-) I'm writing this message from a PII350,
> which isn't too bad but we have hard time trying to get its RAM
> at least above 512MB  (prices for 256MB modules I've
> got from European suppliers are about $1K each).

Pentium class is out of the question.  32 bit address space limits you to
4GB.  In practice, because of how the memory is segmented, you can only put
1GB on any Intel system.  There are patches to hack it up to 2GB, but at the
expense of larger per-process memory usage (IIRC).  Apparently the Xeon
class processors support 36-bit addressing, but in a stupid and archaic
(typical Intel) method of segmentation/bank switching.  There was discussion
yesterday on the kernel list about adding support for this to the kernel,
and the response was generally negative.  You never know though, someone
might write it...

I'm interested to hear a response on the question of using only 64-bit PCI
slots on alpha...  PCI is another Intel design.  People over there have
their brains hardwired to 32-bit...

Also take a look at UltraPenguin Linux on Sparc 64's.  I don't know if they
support more memory, but it's worth a look since it's theoretically
possible.

-- Bob

