> 
> I'd try both as modules.

That's the current plan but since two folks say to go to
the latest tulip instead of the one that comes in 2.2.3,
I'll try that one instead of the current one.

This is the most critical issue since all my stuff is
NFS'd to all systems for access.

> 
> >    to set. If I can't find a working version, I will see
> >    about disabling the onboard 21142 and installing a 21140
> >    based adapter.
> 
> Thats a worst case.

True. That's why I'm not a big fan of built in HW on a
motherboard for video/SCSI/networking. It requires you
to chase drivers vs put in a board you know works. I have 
the 2.2.3 src and will build a kernel. I'll try the XP1000
first but I'm not hopeful that will build any better than
egcs 1.1.2 has. One comment though. After building egcs 1.1.2
on my 164LX, I ran one of my benchmarks. It seems for reasonably
well laid out loops that use mult/add FP, you can get substantial
performance improvement. (from 310 MFLOPS to 456 MFLOPS) Quite
an improvement in the scheduling algorithm for 21164's...

> 
> > 2. I seem to have either heat problems or the memory dimms
> >    are not up to standard. I can't get the latest egcs1.1.2
> 
> Are you using this stock memory?  Did you add your own?  What
> exactly did you add?  What happens if it's stock only?  Any ecc
> memory errors in syslog?

It's the memory that came in the box so I assume it
should work. I plan on bringing a stick over to the
local dealer today and see exactly what it is and if
they have any. They have a tester to see what it is.

I also created and left running a memory hog test that
repeatedly accessed 100MB of memory on a 128MB system 
which ran 8 hours without a miscompare so I'm leaning
away from it being a memory issue. I'm running it now
so that it forces the system to page. We'll see how that
goes.

> 
> >    to go thru a full build. It builds fine on my 164LX. I
> >    get either core dumps at what appear to be random places
> >    or I get a message something like "Unable to dup fd xxxxxxxx
> >    to fd 0" while trying to copy the files to the installed
> >    location. The first location looks obviously bogus.
> >    Anyone seen that before??
> >
> > 3. I added a Symbios 875J based UW SCSI to the system and
> 
> There are a limited number of controllers supported in the bios,
> some ncr, qlogic, and adaptec.  I suspect you might not have
> one thats compatible.  You could just boot off ide.  I'd also
> try the controller in another slot (on bus 0).

I was hoping since the Symbios was NCR it would be supported. I
have some old Symbios SCSI 2 narrow cards I could swap in. The
built in SCSI is Qlogic but is an unhappy camper at the moment.
I'll have to get the mainboard replaced to get that working
again. Anyone know what models are supported or where docs are
that tell?

> 
> >    it appears SRM does not see it or any devices attached to
> >    it. Is there something I can set to get that recognized by SRM?
> >    I'm currently booting off the floppy until I get that fixed.
> >    I'd like to boot from that card. Note AlphaBios does see the
> >    adapter. It is on Bus 0, so I guess it's not sitting across
> >    a PCI bridge or anything odd like that.
> >
> > For your amusement:
> >
> > 996.15 BogoMIPS
> 

Keep in mind the Bogus part of BogoMIPS. It does seem
to indicate though that the 21264 is able to do two instructions
per clock on the timing loop where as the 21164 only does one.

> I'd be more interested in stream numbers:
> http://www.cs.virginia.edu/stream/
> 

The streams tests are similar to parts of the benchmark I ran.
All they are is about the simplest loops you can code. Not
very representative of real code. I have seen old vector code
that is like this though I doubt it's in production anymore. 
But, they do stress memory if you use large enough arrays and 
that I suppose is the point of the tests. Anyway, my tests that 
are similar show nowhere near the speed up Compaq quotes on 
their WWW site. I suspect the XP1000 has slow memory compared 
to the DS20/DP264. Also, it could be gcc/g77 can't do simple 
loops well yet and stall the pipeline. On the somewhat more 
realistic tests, I see about 700 MFLOPS vs 456 MFLOPS on 21164. 
Note the XP1000 is using egcs from RH5.2. I'm hoping egcs 1.1.2 
will push that 700 up to high 800's or low 900's, low 900's 
being preferred...


Wes


