List up! Without newbies in the Alpha Linux community, the community
*will* die!

*Most* of the people on this list are not new to Linux or UNIX type
sytems. But before you post to the axp-list there are few things that
should be done first.

1. Check the man page or specific documentation for your problem.
2. Look in /usr/doc/* (esp. /usr/doc/HOWTO and /usr/doc/HOWTO/mini) for
   more docs.
3. Search the list archives.
   www.alphalinux.org has a active archive and so does
   www.lib.uaa.alaska.edu/archives.html (this one is larger and
   searchable).
4. Ask yourself if this is a general Red Hat Linux question, if so
   redhat-list would be a better place to ask.

For #4 redhat-list will usually get you faster responses (with the
exception of Jay Estabrook of Compaq :-) And the axp-list is for Alpha
*specific* topics anyway.


Instead of flaming newbies please try and point them to good resources to
get them started. If they still can't find an answer to their question,
the redhat-list should be their next stop - unless it is specifically
Alpha related, i.e. problems installing, strage errors, can't do xxx on
Alpha but can on x86, etc.

New comers to Alpha are welcome (we need them)!



My $0.02 worth.


JMHO,

Ron Farrer 
