I think what you have there is a processor machine check - something is
amiss with the way your CPU is talking to the system.  What is suspicious is
that in both cases the PC is identical.  Machine checks of this kind can be
invoked by kernel mode software.  For example, if your kernel is trying to
read from an address outside of your physical memory map, you can get one of
these.  At least, thats what happens in BIOS and firmware.

To trace the problem further, you can look up what function is causing this
exception in the kernel symbol map (if you have one).  For example, you
might find it in the bowels of a device driver, for which there is an
upgrade.

Regards,
Stig Telfer

