yue-feng sun wrote:
> 
> Hello,
> 
>         I have an EXB-8700LT 8mm tape drive attached to Alpha 533 under RH4.2.
> Following the online mt manual, I tried to use high-density format 8500c
> supported on this drive by using
>         mt -f /dev/st0 setdensity 0x8c
> or      mt -f /dev/st0 setdensity 140
> 
> Both didn't work. The command didn't fail but the density still remains
> 0x0 (default) by checking mt -f /dev/st0 status. The default format
> writes 5GB and the 8500c format should write 10 GB which I could not get.
> 
>         Could anyone help on how to use the compressed 8500 density format
> on Linux?
> 
>         Please send email also to sunyf@ldeo.columbia.edu

I've been using 8mm and DLT on RH4.2/5.0/5.2 without any
major trouble. One thing I did notice is that using mt to
set things requires a tape to be inserted in the drive or
it will ignore your request. Also, I suspect the default
mode for your device is 5GB with compression on, which is
the 10GB mode. You really can't write 10GB if the data 
is not compressible.


Wes


