That will be useful.

I don't know about you, but 25% better performance is 
nothing to sneeze at according to my tests with unrolling
the stream loops.

It would be interesting to generate the asm file from an
ev6 tuned DUNIX cc compile and compare it to what egcs is
doing.

Tom, if you can, add whatever flag DUNIX cc needs to
output asm. On gcc, it's -S. Here's the loops with egcs1.0.3
with -O2:

copy vector

$L24:
        ldt $f1,0($2)
        addq $2,8,$2
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,0($3)
        addq $3,8,$3
        bne $1,$L24

vector times scalar:

$L29:
        ldt $f1,0($2)
        mult $f2,$f1,$f1
        addq $2,8,$2
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,0($3)
        addq $3,8,$3
        bne $1,$L29

Add two vectors:

$L34:
        ldt $f1,0($2)
        ldt $f10,0($3)
        addq $3,8,$3
        addt $f1,$f10,$f1
        addq $2,8,$2
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$5,$1
        stt $f1,0($4)
        addq $4,8,$4
        bne $1,$L34

Vector plus scaled vector:

$L39:
        ldt $f1,0($2)
        mult $f2,$f1,$f1
        ldt $f10,0($3)
        addq $3,8,$3
        addt $f10,$f1,$f10
        addq $2,8,$2
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$5,$1
        stt $f10,0($4)
        addq $4,8,$4
        bne $1,$L39

Nice clean easy to read asm at least.

If you specify -funroll-all-loops, you get an 8 way
unroll. Note specifying -funroll-loops does nothing
to the code. I guess egcs doesn't think simple loops
need unrolling!! Sure would be interesting to see what
DUNIX's trick is.


Wes


