> I think the key here is that your gcc output contains no write hints for the
> destination.

This doesn't make a 2x difference in performance -- if this
instruction is actually implemented in the 21264 (I haven't paid
attention; the hints didn't do anything in the 21164), not using it
would make your copy rate 33% lower than using it. This is known as
"write allocate" traffic and the STREAM documentation talks about it.

f90 -tune ev6 does generate wh64 instructions...

It would probably be really hard to teach gcc how to use this
instruction. gcc doesn't know much about vectorization. Of course
memcpy() and friends could have this added by hand.

-- g


