Stig Telfer wrote:
> 
> I think the key here is that your gcc output contains no write hints for the
> destination.  If you're writing a buffer without first reading it, this
> instruction is near-essential; otherwise, the CPU will fetch the destination
> cacheline from memory before you write all over it.  Adding a wh64
> instruction (in theory) allocates a 64-byte cache block without filling from
> memory.  That could explain the 2x shortfall in memory bandwidth you're
> seeing.
> 
> I was hoping to provide an example to test this, but gnu as doesn't seem to
> know the instruction, at least not the version I have (and not with the
> mnemonic in the arch handbook).  Hand-assembling may do the trick for now.
> I wonder how much effort gcc makes to use it?
> 

I patched my gas with Richard's update. It does
accept wh64 opcodes now. I'll see about updating
the asm with it for the 8 way unrolled case. I
do have a comment about how gcc unrolls loops.
It may or may not be useful. In general, when I
hand code unrolled loops, what I thought was the
standard way to do this is apparently NOT how gcc
does it. The general form of unrolls I use in C:

for(i=0; i < n-unroll+1; i+=unroll) {
   a[i]=b[i];
      .
      .
      .
   a[i+unroll-1]=b[i+unroll-1];
}

nleft=n%unroll;
if(nleft > 0)
  for(i=n-nleft; i < n; i++) a[i]=b[i];

It seems gcc inserts compares inside the main loop
to see when to leave it after each piece of the unroll
which seems like a waste of energy to me.

Isn't the above form more efficient cpu-wise??
Seems so since it doesn't waste any cycles checking
when to quit in the loop you really care about.


Wes


