An example of the main loop difference with the above
technique vs what gcc generates:

gcc:

$L24:
        ldt $f1,0($2)
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,0($3)
        beq $1,$L22
        ldt $f1,8($2)
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,8($3)
        beq $1,$L22
        ldt $f1,16($2)
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,16($3)
        beq $1,$L22
        ldt $f1,24($2)
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,24($3)
        beq $1,$L22
        ldt $f1,32($2)
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,32($3)
        beq $1,$L22
        ldt $f1,40($2)
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,40($3)
        beq $1,$L22
        ldt $f1,48($2)
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,48($3)
        beq $1,$L22
        ldt $f1,56($2)
        addq $2,64,$2
        addl $9,1,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f1,56($3)
        addq $3,64,$3
        bne $1,$L24

My example method:
(assume for the example, that the value in $4 is a multiple of 8)

$L24:
        wh64 ($3)
        ldt $f1,0($2)
        ldt $f2,8($2)
        ldt $f3,16($2)
        ldt $f4,24($2)
        ldt $f5,32($2)
        ldt $f6,40($2)
        ldt $f7,48($2)
        ldt $f8,56($2)
        stt $f1,0($3)
        stt $f2,8($3)
        stt $f3,16($3)
        stt $f4,24($3)
        stt $f5,32($3)
        stt $f6,40($3)
        stt $f7,48($3)
        addq $2,64,$2
        addl $9,8,$9
        cmple $9,$4,$1
        stt $f8,56($3)
        addq $3,64,$3
        bne $1,$L24

Bottom opcodes may have better order. It's basically the
bottom of the gcc code. Also, depending on memory latency,
you can start the stt's before you get all the ldt's
done, assuming you can do both an stt and an ldt in the
same cycle.

Seems that should perform quite a bit better than the
gcc loop. Can gcc be setup to do this??

If you seriously want to use wh64, you need to do a
pre-process loop to handle the non-64 byte boundary
at the front, and a post-process loop to handle the
end.

Just some thoughts.


Wes


