> Looks like it uses conditionals to decide when to do wh64.

It has to. You don't know the alignment of the variables at the start
of the loop, so you'd have to test and have a leader and trailer
instead of just a trailer. But if you were writing multiple variables,
undoubtedly they'd have different alignment. So the easiest trick is
to use 1 conditional for each wh64. I don't know if the 21264's branch
prediction is good enough to avoid a bubble for a branch which is
taken 1/8 of the time, but avoiding extra main memory traffic is a
huge win.

We could easily figure out a bit of code that has an if() and one
asm() statement that allows us to do this by hand in egcs-compiled
codes. In order for egcs to generate this automatically, it would have
to know that the loop was big enough to make it worthwhile, and also
that the array in question was not read-before-written in the loop.

-- g


