On Mon, Mar 22, 1999 at 10:57:35PM -0600, Wes Bauske wrote:
> > On Mon, Mar 22, 1999 at 11:14:44AM -0600, Wes Bauske wrote:
> > > Actually, I'd like to get egcs1.1.2 to just build on
> > > the XP1000. I'm seeing a timing dependent bug in the
> > > build process that can lock the system up. Basically,
> > > I get "unable to dup fd xxxxxxxx to fd 0", SEGV's, and
> > > outright lockups.
> > 
> > Jay mentioned (and I loosely concur) the possibility of
> > TLB muckups on EV6.  One way to test this is to build a
> > generic kernel and in arch/alpha/kernel/machvec.h modify
> > 
> > #define DO_EV6_MMU                                                      \
> >         max_asn:                        EV6_MAX_ASN,                    \
> >         mmu_context_mask:               0xfffffffffful,                 \
> > -       mv_get_mmu_context:             ev5_get_mmu_context,            \
> > -       mv_flush_tlb_current:           ev5_flush_tlb_current,          \
> > -       mv_flush_tlb_other:             ev5_flush_tlb_other,            \
> > -       mv_flush_tlb_current_page:      ev5_flush_tlb_current_page
> > +       mv_get_mmu_context:             ev4_get_mmu_context,            \
> > +       mv_flush_tlb_current:           ev4_flush_tlb_current,          \
> > +       mv_flush_tlb_other:             ev4_flush_tlb_other,            \
> > +       mv_flush_tlb_current_page:      ev4_flush_tlb_current_page
> > 
> > Worth a shot anyway...
> > 
> 
> Good shot. I successfully built egcs1.1.2 with this
> patch to 2.2.3. I'll do some more tests to see if it
> continues to behave.
> 
> Does that tell you what might be going wrong??

It means that there are invalid TLB entries live when things go south.

EV4 has an architecture conformance bug that makes ASNs useless
so we are forced to flush the whole TLB when context switching.

EV5 gets ASNs correct, so that isn't necessary.  Moreover, when we
change a process' vm mappings, we can just make the process use a
new ASN, which cuts the number of flushes we must do by 128.

EV6... I dunno.  My HRM appears to be oh so usefully back at the office.



r~

PS: One other thing you could try is to go back to using the ev5 tlb
flush routines, but only use EV5_MAX_ASN.


