Dear fellow Alpha-Linux folks,

I'm trying to upgrade a redhat 5.2 system with kernel 2.0.35 to kernel
2.2.1 (DCG advised me that this was the most stable 2.2 kernel at
present), and I can't get my network to work with the 2.2.1 kernel.
The machine is the server node of a 16-node beowulf cluster.

First, here's the machine:

    From DCG Inc: 
    Alpha 21164a 533 MHz, LX motherboard, 128M SDRAM, 
    2M cache, Ultra-wide Intraserver SCSI controller,
    two Quantum SCSI drives 2Gb ea, Matrox Millenium II w/ 4 Mb
    two NICs: DEC DE500-AA pci 10/100

and the OS:

    RedHat 5.2, 2.2.1 kernel (formerly 2.0.35), booted via SRM and 
    aboot.

One of the NICs is connected directly (via crossover cable) to a
pentium machine.  The other goes to an Ethernet switch and thence
to the other 15 alphas in the cluster.

When I use the 2.0.35 kernel (with Tulip driver), the network is
fine, and I can ping anything from anything.  If I then boot the
system with a 2.2.1 kernel, all pings fail (except for pings of self).
The hardware and network configuration files are identical in the two
cases.  The only difference is the vmlinux.gz used to boot the system.
In configuring the 2.2.1 kernel, I first tried to make it as similar as
possible to the configuration I used for 2.0.35.  When this failed,
I then tried many variant configurations for the 2.2.1, but so far
nothing has worked.  Some of the things I have varied in the kernel
configuration include:

    CONFIG_EXPERIMENTAL
    CONFIG_DE4X5
    CONFIG_DEC_ELCP
    CONFIG_PCI_QUIRKS

and various other things that are probably irrelevant to the network.

I am appending as attachments the working .config I used for 2.0.35
(file config.2.0.35) and one of the many .configs I used for 2.2.1
(file config.2.2.1).

Has anyone else encountered this problem?  Did I do something stupid
in configuring the 2.2.1 kernel?  Should I be using a later kernel
than 2.2.1?  If so, which one?  Does anyone have an LX machine with
a 2.2.x kernel that works with the DE500-AA card?  If so, how did
you configure your kernel?  Any help would be greatly appreciated.

TIA,
Jack Wathey


