On Tue, 23 Mar 1999, Robert Harley wrote:

> This is what I like to hear: Compaq making positive noises about Alpha
> and Linux!
> 
>   http://www.news.com/News/Item/Textonly/0,25,34119,00.html?pfv
> 

If Compaq wants to make real noise with Linux, help optimize gcc/egcs for
the Alpha processor.  And put the updates right back into gcc/egcs unlike
what Intel is doing with Cygnus and the PII/PIII optimizations.
Plus, free up the DU libraries so we can run DU
apps until native Alpha Linux apps are written/ported.
It's amazing how companies all like to keep their own little secrets that
only end up hurting themselves.  It seems so damn simple.  If the hardware
is $5000 and the OS is $1000, but people choose not to buy the whole package
because of the $1000 cost for OS and development environment and not to
mention the yearly licensing fee, ugh.  That's $6000 gross Compaq just
lost.  Don't think this is hypothetical because our lab looked into Alphas
and balked on the cost of the software.  We were not going to be trapped
into paying the yearly licensing fee, even if it was under the university
program.
Now, lets say that Compaq decides to market Alphas with Linux.  The OS is
$0 plus it will run DU software with no loss in performance and I get to
use high performance development environment, and let's say Compaq sells
the boxes for $5500, our lab would have picked up TWO of these.  That would
have been $11,000 gross for Compaq.  Gee, $11,000 vs $0.  Duh?? It doesn't
take a freakin' MBA to figure this one out.  Guess the guys at Digital 
didn't figure it out.
How much would it cost them to do all this?  $0.  The code is out there.
All they have to do is turn it loose to the egcs people and it'll be done
by the next release.  

Jiann-Ming Su                   "People seldom do what they believe in. They do
