On Wed, Mar 24, 1999 at 04:03:13PM -0600, Tom Browder wrote:
> Greg, I'm sure you are correct: it's probably a memory overwrite bug--
> isn't there a free bounds checker out there for C++ (checkers?).  
> I know about dmalloc for C.

Try Electric Fence, an old tool by Bruce Perens.  While
it's nowhere near as extensive a tool as say Bounds Checker,
it will almost always catch an errant write to memory you don't
own, or a double free() or delete, etc.  I'm not sure where
the latest version is, but it doesn't require any code
changes.  Just recompile and link with -lefence and it'll
use its own special malloc().  Run your program in gdb (and
grab 4.17 if you don't have it; it's about 2 whole years newer
than 4.16) and your program should throw a signal right when
memory is getting stomped on.

-- 
Shaw Terwilliger (twig@advancenet.net)


