Folks,

I'm working on an Avalon (Beowulf) Cluster based on 164LX motherboards
running Red Hat Linux Version 5.2.  The slave CPUs are "headless", viz.
no video cards.  When a slave CPU boots up, I communicate to it through
a Cyclades network card connected to the slave through it's serial port
(for example, master: /dev/cub7 --> slave: /dev/ttyS0).  To access the
slave CPU as console from the master CPU I invoke the following:

          xterm -n nd_07 -T nd_07 -e cu -l /dev/cub7 &

This creates an Xterm window on the master's console.  Normally 
everything works fine except during reset bootup when I'm reconfiguring
the AlphaBIOS.  When invoking the various menu commands of the AlphaBIOS
it is necessary to use cryptic keystrokes because of the strange
keyboard mapping, i.e.

		I type "Ctrl-b" when it wants "F2".
		I type "Ctrl-i" when it wants "Tab".
		I type "Ctrl-v" when it wants "Insert".
		            -- etc. --

Most of the time this is just annoying and not a real problem because I
have my magic decoder ring that tells me what almost all of the cryptic
keystrokes are.

This true EXCEPT when the AlphaBIOS wants to see:

			PageUP
			PageDown

So this now leads to my question:

+----------------------------------------------------------------+
| What are the cryptic keystrokes that I type when the AlphaBIOS |
| wants to see a PageUp or a PageDown?                           |
+----------------------------------------------------------------+


                           Thanks in advance,

			      Gary Allen




