With regards to your message at 01:34 PM 03-26-99 -0600, Jiann-Ming Su.
Where you stated:
>http://www.zdnet.com/pcweek/stories/news/0,4153,1014215,00.html
>
>Jiann-Ming Su                   "People seldom do what they believe in.
They do

Yes, it is a tough business for the NT Alpha sellers.
Good thing that the Linux business is brisk and the companies that support
it are having record sales.
Of course that is not what a "Wintel-centric" magazine writer like PCWeek
writes about!

Sucks to be them...   ;^}

Some of us who have been in the Alpha business long before Enorex came
along and told us "how to sell Alpha computers" are now sitting back and
smiling at the irony.

Of course this article is also reporting on the "Compaq OEMs" who are now
essentailly non-existant. As such the article is really about "last years
news".
 Those of us in the Alpha OEM market are now mainly Alpha Processor
resellers, as Compaq closed the former Digital Semiconductor division long
ago, and have now sold the TOEM division to another (undisclosed) company.
Rumour has it that this may be IBM, and personally, I hope so. IBM has a
number of OEM divisions which are very good to work with, such as their
storage division and their printer division. If this turns out to be true I
hope to see some competition at the manufacturing and distribution level,
and perhaps better prices and more product releases for the Alpha community.

Notwithstanding, to look at Compaq for solutions is now a much weaker
concept than in the past, but fortunately for us API is picking up the
slack pretty fast.

Of course, in the meantime we have Compaq out there selling XP1000 machines
at a very aggressive price, with only a 7% margin for their dealers. We
hope they are truly prepared to support their Linux customers, because if
the DO succeed in driving out the "nasty OEMs" they will become the sole
source of support for you.

I don't think this is going to happen however, as we are seeing that the XP
is really not a great machine, with severe memory bandwidth and quality
control issues.
And in a couple of months we shall see the newer SlotB designs from Alpha
Processor and AMD affiliates coming to market, at which time the 21264 will
become an affordable alternative for the market.

And if I will offend anyone with what I say, my response is  "oh well".
 That is how we see it, and we make a living at this game.


