With regards to your message at 08:41 PM 03-26-99 -0500, Jeff DeMaagd.
Where you stated:
>
>
>On Fri, 26 Mar 1999, Maurice Hilarius wrote:
>
>> With regards to your message at 01:34 PM 03-26-99 -0600, Jiann-Ming Su.
>> Where you stated:
>> >http://www.zdnet.com/pcweek/stories/news/0,4153,1014215,00.html
>> 
>> Yes, it is a tough business for the NT Alpha sellers.
>> Good thing that the Linux business is brisk and the companies that support
>> it are having record sales.
>
>So why did VA Research dump Alpha sales?
Because it is "main stream" and more and more they are a "general" supplier.
Also, they , like most of the other "traditional" Alpha OEMs cut their
deals with Digital. Once Digital began grooming itself for resale to
Compaq, and started dumping out of OEM business, it was no longer
attractive to them. Besides, VAResearch has a major capital investment from
Intel, and are under an agreement with Intel to develop/support Linux on
Merced.

>
>> Some of us who have been in the Alpha business long before Enorex came
>> along and told us "how to sell Alpha computers" are now sitting back and
>> smiling at the irony.
>
>> And in a couple of months we shall see the newer SlotB designs from Alpha
>> Processor and AMD affiliates coming to market, at which time the 21264 will
>> become an affordable alternative for the market.
>
>Any word on the projected prices?  I realize that the 264's are the
>fastest and most scalable CPUs in the world, they aren't going to hit
>mainstream until they beat K6 / Celeron A in price / performance.  As much
>as I like my present Miata, I'm not going to upgrade to either the XP1000
>or DP264 type systems when they cost 10,000 USD.  Price them at about a
>high-end Macintosh range of prices (2 - 4k) then I am listening.
The uniprocessor 21264 at 600MHz should retail at about the same price as
an LX did when it cam out 18 months ago. About $4000 for a fully loaded
workstation. And then prices will drop slowly, as usual. At least that is
my prediction..

And the beat goes on.

The main thing to see is that the biggest problem Alpha has had has been
single-source, and a non-commodity status. As this changes I think we shall
see good things.
Indeed, over the last 2 years prices have dropped way down to readily
compete against X86 products.

>
>Why is Samsung (API?) waiting to make the next generation products?

Waiting? They are working very hard to get this to market.
Matter of fact most of the Alphas being produced are API chips, not Compaq
from the Intel fab in Hudson. Heck, Campaq is buying a lot of their CPUs
from Samsung now.
There are a few folks who pop up on this list working for API on this.
Take Stig Telfer, for example. Or Rich Payne of  Alphalinux.org, who tells
me he is now a sysadmin for API.
But don't expect them to say too much. They have jobs to do, and their
bosses decide who is saying what to who!

All I can say is have a look at the Alpha Processor Inc pages. There is
quite a bit there already.
www.alpha-processor.com/

There is a pdf doc there with some announcements, and more coming.




