Content-Type: text/plain; charset=US-ASCII
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit

The FAQ now says:

> 12.4 Can Linux/Alpha Run Without Graphics Card?
> 
> The firmware can be controlled over a serial line terminal at 9600
> Baud and it is possible to boot Linux/Alpha in that manner, but Linux
> itself does not currently support headless boots. There are kernel
> patches floating on the net that add this functionality, however.

This is obselete. This could replace it:

12.4 Can Linux/Alpha Run Without A Graphics Card?

Starting with version 2.2 of the kernel, it is easy to run a headless
Linux box. There are two sets of issues: dealing with the firmware,
and dealing with Linux.

Linux
=====

To run Linux headless, you need to configure a new kernel, answering
"yes" to the CONFIG_SERIAL_CONSOLE question. Then you have to also
make sure that /dev/console points to the appropriate device, i.e.

rm /dev/console ; mknod /dev/console c 4 64

If you have the machine already installed and booted with a graphics
card, this is fairly easy to arrange. If you want to install headless,
then you need to unpack your ramdisk image and change /dev/console
there. However, after the RedHat install process, for example,
/dev/console on your harddisk will be wrong again. So you will have to
boot again from floppy, mount the hard disk, and change /dev/console.

Firmware
========

All versions of SRM and AlphaBios after 5.66 (?) supports serial
consoles. Connect a serial cable to com1 at 9600 baud, 8-N-1. I've
noticed that if Digital Unix "quick start" is installed, it won't
accept any keyboard input once it's going. To prevent that, type a few
^C's before Digital Unix Boots.

-- g


